== Contextual Inquiry <section:contextualinquiry>
The contextual inquiry was conducted in collaboration with Brain Stimulation and was used to inform the needs and design decisions of this study's co-design session. The contextual inquiry resulted in three main themes: (1) Phases of Rehabilitation, (2) Current System and Technology Benefits & Limitations, and (3) Ideal Digitalized Rehabilitation.

=== Phases of Rehabilitation <section:phasesoftherapy> 
The general flow of therapy as per the participants (P3-P5) from the case study of Brain Stimulation, therapists went through three main phases: (1) Assessment & Goal-Setting Level, (2) Session level, and (3) Task level (see @phases). The rehabilitation is always initiated with a meeting where both experienced issues and goals are discussed together with the patient. Only then does the therapist proceed to performing activities with the patient such as RehAtt® DiSTRO cognitive assessment tests, and daily activity tests to assess the type of issues the patient has and the severity (_Phase 1_).

#quote(block:true)["I perform cognitive tests and function tests to ensure if it is cognitive issues alone or not. Then ask about family and personal life. Ask about the day so far. Daily chores and activities are checked [e.g. cleaning, cooking, brushing teeth, showering] as well" (P5)]

Once the assessment is finished, the therapist then creates a therapy plan with the agreed goals in mind, and creates a session plan (_Phase 2_) which is most often used throughout the entire rehabilitation due to the limited number of available exergames. During the _rehabilitation session_ (_Phase 3_), the therapist adjusts game parameters, pauses/continues, and changes task/game based on the patient's physical and emotional condition. During the _session_ and _task level_, the therapist also takes notes regarding what was done and how it went for the patient. These notes are regularly analyzed and compared to track progress and update assessment and therapy plan. 

//_"I use the notes to see how the patient is feeling that day and adapt it [session plan & therapy plan] accordingly" - P3_

#quote(block: true)["I use the notes to see how the patient is feeling that day and adapt it [session plan & therapy plan] accordingly" (P3)]

Besides a few notable observations during _session level_ and _task level_ (see @phases), all of the findings matched with the observation that was conducted with one participant (P3). The additional findings were mainly in the form of therapist-patient interaction which seemed to be a necessity for the _rehabilitation session_ to go smoothly. The main interaction was periodical conversing to keep the patient relaxed and get feedback to operate from, while simultaneously giving the patient enough time to focus on their tasks. The feedbacks included from "when to rest", and "for how long to rest", to "if the task was too easy or too difficult", and "if they wanted to change to a different task". This feedback was then used to adjust game parameters such as the difficulty, adding objects with extra points to motivate the patient, or completely changing task. This subjective patient information was deemed to be very important for therapists to provide personalized quality care to their patients. 

Another observation was how the therapist took handwritten notes on paper rather than inputting it in the RehAtt® DiSTRO or  RehAtt® XR. This hindered the therapist's session evaluation process as it fragmented the observation notes during session such as patient motivation, future therapy adjustments, feedbacks, thus disconnecting it from the performance data, patient progress, and session histories provided by the RehAtt® XR. This insight was used to add a fourth phase in the rehabilitation process, namely "_Post-Session Evaluation_" (See @phases). One last observation note was how the therapist configured both before and in real-time during the _rehabilitation session_. This insight was used to introduce _temporality_ as one of the dimensions in the co-design session activity (See @codesign) to determine in which phase of the rehabilitation that therapists want to pre-configure or configure in real-time, and how the system's agency distribution should be designed to support that.

Based off of all the above accumulated findings on therapists rehabilitation process which also largely matched with previous research *on the rehabilitation process* (see @therapistinterfaces), the final rehabilitation process used for this study included the following four main phases (see @phases): (1) Assessment & Goal-Setting, (2) Session Planning, (3) Rehabilitation Session, and (4) Post-Session Evaluation. 

=== Current System and Technology Benefits & Limitations <dimension-interactioncost>

All participants (P1-P5) during the contextual inquiry preferred exergames for personalized rehabilitation, where the main limitations reported were technical related. As expected, the general opinion among the participants was that exergames for rehabilitation is more beneficial as it allows you as a therapist to instantly review all necessary data while observing, making it easier for assessment, decision-making and collaboration with other colleagues. Another repeated but expected finding was how exergames gives the therapist the ability to personalize rehabilitation in various phases, while simultaneously appearing more fun and motivating for the patient. The phases mentioned stretched from the ability to create personalized therapy plans to keeping track of and comparing patient data over time, personalize difficulty accordingly, and appoint various exergames depending on the problem that a patient has. One participant (P1) also highlighted how XR exergames in particular is more effective as it allows the patient to perform high repetitions in simulations that are similar to real-life without being boring like in traditional rehabilitation.

#quote(block:true)["[XR] Exergames that are evidence-based and developed with clinical therapy in mind tend to improve patients states" (P1) ]

#quote(block: true)["... [In exergames] the strength is the flexibility. The strength is really to be able to personalize" (P4)]

While the overall perception of technology and exergames for rehabilitation was positive, connection issues between the therapist monitoring and control tool as well as the XR-exergame device (components of RehAtt® XR) in particular was surprisingly consistently reported. Although this is not the main focus of this study, it still gives insights into the potential existing problems of other similar systems used for rehabilitation through exergames. Other technical issues related to tools abruptly crashing or updating during usage, leading to affected _rehabilitation sessions_. These technical issues were perceived as highly disruptive as it would result in time consumption, thereby reducing time available for the actual _rehabilitation session_. This was a major concern for all the participants (P1-P5) since the rehabilitation field is already short on time, sometimes only having 20-30 minutes to give to a patient. Subsequently, _interaction cost_ was included as a probe dimension for the co-design session (see @codesign) to be able to determine how agency distribution should be distributed across the four rehabilitation phases (see @phases) while being time-efficient and effortless.

#quote(block: true)["... [Rehabilitation] Sessions length range from 20mins to 1 hour" (P3)]

#quote(block: true)["I cannot give that [time] to the technology because I have the patient just for 30 minutes" (P4)]

=== Ideal Digitalized Rehabilitation <dimension-granularity>

The participants mentioned the goal of rehabilitation technology is to provide fun, motivating and evidence-based rehabilitation to the patient, as well as to move the entire field towards precision medicine. Access to rehabilitation was also pointed out as a benefit to using exergame-based rehabilitation technologies as it could enable remote rehabilitation. One participant (P3) elaborated how “_when patient have to travel a lot to come to rehab, they are absolutely exhausted when they come to the rehabilitation_”, emphasizing the value of remote rehabilitation technology. 

The participants, however, mentioned that technology is not a replacement to rehabilitation, but a complementary evidence-based tool to support them. The participants hoped that as technological development progresses, these rehabilitation systems would evolve to be simpler, like an app, instead of a full kit that requires experts for initial setup. The participants also wanted to have a system that included both assessment and rehabilitation in the same system rather than as individual tools. Participant (P2) specifically, also mentioned how the system should be adaptable to the therapist's workflow and rehabilitation domain: 

#quote(block: true)["The product should not be a fixed, but a flexible tool for therapists. The tool should be able to be used in many different ways to fit various rehabilitation purposes/preferences." (P2)]

When asked about interface, the participants wanted simpler settings and exergame parameters, as well as simpler connection between the XR-tool, and therapists' monitoring and control tool (components of RehAtt® XR). Although they preferred simple difficulty adjustments over finer controls for exergames, they instead wanted more control of additional attributes that could manipulate the game to provide individualized care. One example of this is more levels and implementation of level grading to know where in the difficulty spectrum a patient is to adjust it accordingly. This finding helped us generate _granularity_ as another dimension, expanding it to reflect not only settings or levels, but also information architecture of the rehabilitation system as a whole, for both the pilot study and the co-design session (see @codesign).

Interestingly, when inquired about "who adjusts the settings and exergame parameters" between themselves and the AI-supported system, the participants preferred hybrid agency despite wanting to be in full control. One participant (P1) clearly illustrated this contradiction through conducted research on self-rehabilitation with minimal therapists supervision which was found to be successful, despite therapists advocating for full control and presence. This finding reinforced the need to focus on the _agency in reasoning_ and _agency in execution_ as the primary dimensions (See Pilot Study Adjustment 4). 

== Co-Design Sessions
The co-design sessions were conducted one-on-one with 4 occupational therapists and 1 physiotherapist. The sessions resulted in five main themes: (1) Desired System, (2) Conditional Control (3) The Paradox in Details, (4) Human-Human Interaction, and (5) Digitalized Future Rehabilitation.

=== Desired Agency in Phases
All participants (P4-P8) were tasked with designing their own system throughout all phases of rehabilitation (see @designsystem) by assigning the scenarios (see @codesign) to each of the four phases: (1) Assessment & Goal-Setting, (2) Session Planning, (3) Rehabilitation Session, and (4) Post-Session Evaluation (see @phases). The scenarios were: (S1) a system with full human agency of the rehabilitation process without system support, (S2) a system that provides recommendations, (S3) a system which requires instructions as inputs by the therapists, and (S4) a system with full automation with therapists-on-the-loop. Each participant was informed to use one or more scenario cards as they see fit for each phase. The participants were also informed that they could use the same scenario card for all phases if they desired. 

@systemdesign displays how the participants distributed each of the scenarios among the four phases during the designing of the desired system (See @designsystem). During the _assessment & goal-setting phase_, the participants preferred Full Human Agency (_S1_) and Prompt System (_S3_), both of which provided full autonomy and control of decision-making to the therapists without assistance from the system. For _Session Planning_, therapists preferred assistance from system either for recommendations on clinical reasoning preferring (_S2_), or for execution of the decisions (_S3_). The results indicated therapists would prefer to automate the actual rehabilitation sessions either partially, in reasoning only (_S2_) or fully, in both reasoning and execution (_S4_). Similarly, the therapists seem to prefer partial or fully automated system to evaluate and reflect after the session is completed with results indicating preference for recommendation system (_S2_), Prompt System (_S3_) and *Full System Agency* (_S4_). 

The figure also displays how many times each scenario was picked in total, where the system that provides recommendation (_S2_) was most preferred among the participants, while the *fully autonomous system* (S4) was least preferred. One interesting finding was the Prompt System (_S3_) was picked second most to the Recommendation System (_S2_), despite being the most dislikeable during all co-design sessions. 

#quote(block: true)["That [scenario 3] sounds like a lot of work. No, I got tired just of the idea that, Oh my God, no, it's too. That's too much." (P5)]

#figure(
  image("../images/barchart.png"),
  caption: [Cumulative results of "Desired System" activity in co-design Sessions]
) <systemdesign>
#linebreak()

The @systemdesign also suggests that the participants do not want fully automated system for _Assessment & Goal-Setting_ nor in _Session Planning_ as they need more control in early stages to provide individualized rehabilitation as P5 describes below:

#quote(block: true)["I would like to understand what the impairment are, I want to have like full control. I want to test some different things and then I don't want to like a computer to decide." (P5)]

During each scenario walk-through, all co-design participants were also asked at which phase they would find a scenario to be most useful before they got to design their own system. Out of the five co-design participants, only two participants (P4, P7) resulted in conflicting discrepancies. P4 had elaborated that the system with Full Human Agency (S1) would be the most useful for _session planning_ and _post-session evaluation_ at first, then changed their mind and picked _rehabilitation session_. When P4 however designed their own system, they assigned the Full Human Agency (S1) to _Assessment & Goal-Setting_ and nothing else, despite knowing they could use the same scenario for all phases. P7 on the other hand, had appointed the Recommendation System (_S2_) to be most useful for _Assessment & Goal-Setting_, yet only picked the Prompt System (_S3_) for _Assessment & Goal-Setting_ when designing the desired system. P7 also did this despite knowing they could combine several scenarios for the same phase as well. 

=== Conditional Control
The participants mentioned that as a therapist, control over rehabilitation is important, yet are still willing to hand over control to AI-supported systems, but only under certain circumstances. The preferences were conditional and largely dependent on time pressure, level of trust in the system, type of decision being made, *and longer-term professional outlook.*

The participants emphasized that autonomy over clinical decision-making was integral to being a therapist as every patient and every session is different, and having this control allows them to cater better to the patient needs in that moment. The participants compared having options to adjust rehabilitation parameters to control, make and enact the decision to the feeling of having autonomy and control over rehabilitation process.

  #quote(block: true)[“I like to be in control so and every person that I meet is different and every day is different.” (P4)]

Other participants (P8, P6) didn’t feel comfortable passively accepting system recommendations (Scenario 2) and stressed that they needed to be responsible for the clinical decisions. For P7, having full control in the system is similar to their current rehabilitation practice, noting _“would feel like therapist doing therapist work which it is supposed to do”_ while P8, also strongly advocated that clinical-reasoning needs to be led by therapists. Trust in the system was therefore an important precondition to let go of control entirely. This delegation of control was seen as conditional and the threshold for full delegation was high. Trust was described as something built over time and based on the results the system consistently produced.  

  #quote(block: true)[“You would have to be very comfortable with the system that you knew that it was all that it was equally or better than yourself.” (P6)]

Participants also mentioned that relying too heavily on the system would end up reducing their clinical reasoning skills, especially novice therapists who are still developing their own skills, to a point where it would hinder thinking for themselves. They elaborated that clinical reasoning is as much about experience and learning as it is about skill and knowledge. However, when participants were introduced to therapist control alongside automation, they were more accepting of the shared control. The most preferred model for this automation and control was a system which proposes options and clinicians deciding to accept, adjust or reject them (S2). The feeling of being in control was more important if the system took a part of either reasoning or execution. 

#quote(block: true)[“As long as I can adjust if I want to, of course, then it could be a good alternative.” (P5)]

In contrast, three out of the five participants (P4, P6, P8) did not prefer a fully autonomous system (_S4_), even if they were able to override rehabilitation parameters. They associated full automation with a loss of control over the rehabilitation process and expressed concern about how this would affect their role as a therapists and the patient-therapist relationship. However, two participant (P5, P7) were largely positive about the fully autonomous system (S4) as long as they could override, elaborating that their work would be easier this way and would allow them to focus more on patient goals instead. 

#quote(block: true)[“If this was the main therapy, I would have a problem with this ... My opinion wouldn’t change even if I could override things”  (P6)]

Even across scenarios, the participants agreed on a hybrid pattern of control of pre-planning supported by live adjustments during the session with patient. These adjustments were fine-tuning rather than a complete replanning but were still viewed as important to account for patient fatigue, pain or day-to-day variations. These feeling remained consistent across all scenarios except the fully autonomous system (_S4_).
  
#quote(block: true)[“Every great plan can crumble quite quickly depending on like, say, fatigue, pain.” (P8)]

Additionally, similar to the results from the contextual inquiry, time was again a central factor, this time in regards to how much control participants were willing to let go of. Time was described as a luxury in healthcare that competed with realities of clinical practice with P8 noting _“time constraint is probably one of our biggest things that stop us from kind of delivering like better therapy”_. Under this time pressure, all participants said they would be willing to accept the system's plans and parameters, even if they were imperfect for the moment. This was preferred over interrupting the _rehabilitation session_ if the required time or effort was too high and as long as there was no harm to the patient. 

#quote(block: true)[“I would say yes, because I'm human and… sometimes you don't have the time or the effort to do it” (P4)]

This trade-off had a clear limit though, as the therapists still prioritized patient’s care if they believed additional time or effort would significantly provide better outcomes for the patient. 
  
#quote(block: true)[“No, extra control is not worth the time, unless it gives a better outcome” (P7)]
 
=== The Paradox in Details
There was an underlying tension on how high the granularity of the information presented by the system or provided to the system should be. Higher degree of detail was simultaneously seen as a route to understand and as a cognitive and temporal burden. However, a clear pattern of preference was noticed for the Full Human Agency (S1). Each participant (P4-P8) presented a clear preference for lesser degree of detail and default interface with less information with the option to access additional information when necessary. They wanted combination of presets with manual adjustments to provide effect individualized rehabilitation.

#quote(block: true)[“It would be nice to have some of the features hidden or taken care of by the computer.” (P4)]
  
#quote(block: true)[“As long as I can see the progression and can make changes that are important or when needed.” (P7)]

Similarly, all participants (P4-P8) also described they prefer to give instructions to the system (S3) in a less detailed manner with a focus on functional instructions rather than fine-grained instructions, otherwise higher time and effort would likely be required to provide detailed instructions. They also wanted recommendations (S2) to include enough explanatory details to understand why a suggestion was made, as long as it does not become excessive. This suggests a preference for a balanced design that combines simplicity, focus on important informations, preset prompts, and manual adjustments for more technical experienced users.

#quote(block: true)[”In an easy way say it or type it then it would be good.. otherwise you you lose the contact because you still have to be there with the patient” (P5)]
  
#quote(block: true)[“You might want to wish to improve (the range of motion) but I don't think the system should tell you how much to increase by.” (P8)] 

Moreover, participants (P4-P8) saw detailed output as most useful in moments of disagreement or uncertainty, which required further investigation to check its validity or to learn. When there was a discrepancies between system reasoning and their own, one participant (P6) explained “_you would probably have to like think about why would it say that why would the system say that of course you would have to make that reflection_”. Regardless of the composition of agency between human-AI, the higher degree of granularity was important either to understand why there was difference in reasoning, or to learn why the system provided recommendation or what changes the system made based on their instructions. The participants viewed the high granularity to explain the system’s reasoning and system’s processes as a positive requirement. They mentioned this framing was particularly valuable for novice therapists to learn reasoning behind therapeutic decisions. 
  
#quote(block: true)[“It would be nice to know the why the recommendation is set.” (P7)]
  
When the system performed reasoning autonomously (S2, S4), participants noted they wanted more details rather than less. One participant (P8) noted “_I would wanna be seeing the reasoning and whether or not I would agree with it. I think the level of detail as to why would be very important._”. The participants were clearly interested in understanding why behind the reasoning, however, the level of detail they preferred were still more diagnostic with P6 exemplifying “_It would have to explain the reason very clearly, what kind of cognitive ability is lacking, severity and why, because there are many different cognitive functions._”, rather than fine-level parameters with P5 noting “_but not too specific like changing a setting with 3cm, I'm not that data interested._”

Overall, participants did not want less information but more curated information. High degree of detail felt like a burden when required as input or displayed by default, but was preferred when it was required for justification, serving as a means for reflection or for surfacing something the participants might have missed. 


=== Human-Human Interaction
Another main theme that repeatedly arose throughout all co-design sessions and across all above themes was the importance of a good patient-therapist relationship. All co-design participants (P4-P8) asserted that despite the benefits of digitalized rehabilitation through exergames, it still does not cover all the necessities for a successful rehabilitation. The two most significant necessities for successful rehabilitation which technology alone can not cover for are, (1) accounting for the remaining hours of a patient's day, and (2) making the patient feel seen, heard and understood throughout the rehabilitation process. 

#quote(block: true)["Rehabilitation process is not always linear that goes from progress to progress. So the contradiction is not unexpected. Because you're going to have dips." (P5)]

This became even more conclusive when all participants stated they would rather see the patient over additional quantitative data enabled by the AI-supported system. The participants argued that not having access to observe the patient, meant the therapist could not give or receive feedback, and therefore not be able to take patient's requests and state of mind into account. The participants emphasis on human connection and its impact on the therapy was well articulated by P8:

#quote(block: true)["Digitalized therapy can be like an adjunct, it can't fully take away from what we do, that kind of patient connection and the utilization of clinical reasoning and skills on site." (P8)]

One participant in particular (P6) also illuminated the issue of not being able to determine whether the patient had actually done the assigned exercises and improved, or had another person do the exercises for them if they were to never meet the patient for regular checkups. The participant further elaborated that this could increase the chances of incorrect assessment which could lead to detrimental consequences for both the patient and the therapist. Regardless, the participant could still see the potential of some remote rehabilitation with more infrequent meetings as: 

#quote(block: true)["it could be a good option for somebody that doesn't can't see an occupational therapy regularly or have to travel a very long time to see one" (P6)]

All of the above mentioned reasoning were persistently connected to how essential they were to establishing better motivation and trust between the patient and the therapist, thus increasing the likelihood of consistent rehabilitation and correct clinical assessment. This difficulty is clearly illuminated below:

#quote(block: true)["From my experience I would say the hardest thing would be to get the patient to actually do the rehabilitation" (P7)]

There were however some contextual situations where the participants (P4-P8) could consider not seeing the patient at all. Those were only if, (1) the system was ethical and secure for remote use and if the patient would agree to being recorded for the purpose of observation and feedback, (2) whether or not the patient is in need of frequent feedback, further emphasizing the importance of not losing the human interaction in rehabilitation, and (3) trust that the system would provide effective and correct rehabilitation.

=== Digitalized Future Rehabilitation

The participants were uncertain whether the type of medium for digitalized rehabilitation would affect the workflow in their rehabilitation process, although all participants agreed AI-supported digitalized rehabilitation is the future. One participant (P4) with 25 years of experience as an OT illuminated the change in attitude towards digital rehabilitation: 

#quote(block: true)["As an OT, when I started, it was frowned upon, and ... now, everyone is doing computers or tablets ... it's a huge part of the activities that we do every day" (P4)]

While every participant agreed that rehabilitation is moving towards AI-supported digital systems, the level of digitalization varied from simple digitalized assessments (P5) to fully AI rehabilitation systems (P4, P6). P6 hoped that the access to rehabilitation for people who would otherwise not receive rehabilitation would one of the outcome of more technologically advanced rehabilitation systems. P8 on the other hand hoped that the technology is more helpful than harmful and that we do not lose our patient-centeredness because of it. 

#quote(block: true)[“I don’t want technology to take away from the humanization of what we do, but it could definitely assist us in being more thoughtful to aid that clinical reasoning and help us be more efficient.” (P8)]

Regardless of the participants positive outlook on digitalized rehabilitation, three of the participants still mentioned a few concerns with AI-supported digitalized rehabilitation. P6 noted that the technology has to be at least similar if not better than current process to shift towards a new technology. In contrast, P5 was rather concerned with the impending stress when technology does not function as intended. P7 on the other hand noted the time consuming learning curve that would be required to learn the new technology. 

The preferences for future system interactions and technology mediums also varied among the participants. Participants showed clear preference towards traditional system interaction methods like form-based interactions (e.g writing, select input, buttons etc.) for planning therapy and sessions. When prompted about voice interaction, their concerns were more technical like noisy environment, voice recognition, accents and privacy rather than ease of use. While the preferences for technology mediums stretched from Wii-like technology with screen and motion sensors to XR glasses, they always preferred technology mediums they were already familiar with and in alignment with their respective rehabilitation process. Despite that, tablet, was the main medium that was with one exception, repeatedly mentioned by all participants (P4, P6-P8) as they are both familiar, easy to carry around and supports their workflow. This is given the fact they often have to move between rooms and may have to care for multiple patients at a time. 

#quote(block: true)[“Something more portable. Since I have to go room to room” (P5)]

On the other hand, participant P4 emphasized that the quality of the data and accuracy is more important than the specific technology used. Similarly, two participants (P6, P8) mentioned they would like to see other assessment data and the ability of comparing current patient data with standardized performance from similar patients to make better therapeutic decisions. 